WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.768
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:02.768 --> 00:00:05.270
and comedian you know from shows
like "Rescue Me"

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:06.673
and the "Ice Age" movies.

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.374
You can see him in "The Moodys,"

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:10.109
which airs
Thursday nights on Fox.

00:00:10.109 --> 00:00:11.110
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.411
-Get this, okay?

00:00:12.411 --> 00:00:14.948
The guys, they want me to move
up to the senior league.

00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:16.549
-Well, you have been
getting injured a lot.

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:17.650
-Mm.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.486
-I leave it all out
on the ice, honey.

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:20.653
That's what I do.
Okay?

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:22.521
-Mm.
-Okay.

00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:25.024
You're making noises, Grace.
-Oh, no, no, no.

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:28.128
It's actually very normal
what you're going through.

00:00:28.128 --> 00:00:32.399
We struggle with transition
as we mature.

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:34.466
-Okay.
You can go in the office now.

00:00:34.466 --> 00:00:37.103
Actually, you know what?
Better yet, I'm going upstairs.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:38.905
Three steps at a time, okay?

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.239
[ Laughter ]

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:42.208
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Denis Leary.

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:45.011
How are you, Denis?
-Hey, Seth.

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:47.413
What's up, man?
-It's so great to see you.

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:49.916
I want to talk about the show,
but I also want to ask --

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:54.353
I know your grown children moved
back home during the pandemic.

00:00:54.353 --> 00:00:56.990
Was that a wonderful thing
for your family,

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.793
or did it
add some stress to things?

00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:04.030
-Uh, it was both, actually.
[ Laughing ]

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:05.931
I'm not the --
I can't be only person

00:01:05.931 --> 00:01:08.768
who's said this to you, right?
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:10.503
-How old are your kids now?
Your kids are tiny.

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.905
-Yeah, 3 and 5.
So it's really just been --

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:15.574
it's been mostly upside for us,
because they --

00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:17.977
you know, they're just happy
to have us around.

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:19.512
-Right, and also you're --
are you guys, like,

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:21.280
doing some kind of
homeschooling thing?

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.283
-Yeah, you know -- this will --

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:25.952
I will not be the first person
to say this either --

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:28.087
most of that burden
has fallen on my wife,

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:29.823
who has been incredible with it.

00:01:29.823 --> 00:01:32.058
I'm just getting the best of it.

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:33.693
-Yeah, right, okay.

00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:37.297
Well, try homeschooling
grown adults,

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:40.833
'cause that's basically
what we had to do.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:42.835
To get them all home at
the beginning of the pandemic,

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:45.404
we felt like everybody felt,
which is just so glad

00:01:45.404 --> 00:01:48.707
to have the family together and
it was gonna be cool and fun.

00:01:48.707 --> 00:01:52.811
And that lasted for probably
about two weeks.

00:01:52.811 --> 00:01:55.882
And then, you know,
it's human nature --

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:58.184
You're gonna find this out
much later from now --

00:01:58.184 --> 00:01:59.786
but, you know,
at a certain point, your kids,

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:01.955
when they become teenagers,
they don't think you're cool.

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:06.259
But as my wife and I found out,
when they're home with you

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:08.528
for an extended period
when they're in their late 20s,

00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:10.897
they really
don't think you're cool.

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:12.732
[ Laughter ]
As a matter of fact,

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:15.401
they actually start
to treat you like roommates.

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:17.003
That's what happened.
We were like --

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:19.305
We woke up one day,
and it was like there was

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:20.939
you know, five roommates
living in the house.

00:02:20.939 --> 00:02:22.341
That's what was going on.

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:25.845
So they were just
eating everything, and like --

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:28.781
just like -- you know,
like we were equals to them.

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:31.050
You know what I mean?
I'll never forget this.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.686
Probably about two weeks in,
I was watching something

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:35.588
on television
in the late afternoon,

00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:38.491
and my daughter walked in
and said,

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:41.327
"So we're about to vote
on dinner."

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:43.929
And I was like, "What?
We're gonna vote?"

00:02:43.929 --> 00:02:45.297
And she's like, "Yeah,
and then we're gonna vote on

00:02:45.297 --> 00:02:46.899
what we're gonna
binge watch tonight."

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:48.500
And I'm like "Vote?

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.669
There's no voting.
What are you talking about?

00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:54.006
This isn't a democracy.
This is a monarchy, okay?

00:02:54.006 --> 00:02:55.908
That's what this is.
I'm the King.

00:02:55.908 --> 00:02:57.376
Actually I'm not the King.
I'm the Prince.

00:02:57.376 --> 00:02:58.811
My wife is the Queen."
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:00.112
-"Actually,
I don't even have a vote.

00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:02.715
Nobody has a vote.
Your mother says what goes on."

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:05.985
So that's when they learned,
like, there's no voting.

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:07.219
Your mother decides,

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:10.122
and then we all do
what she wants to do, okay?

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:12.825
Unbelievable.
-It doesn't surprise me

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:16.462
that you would choose projects
that are about family, as well,

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:17.630
which this show is.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:20.499
This is a limited series,
second season of it.

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:21.834
It revolves --
It's a family,

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:23.436
and it revolves around
one event.

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:25.872
First season was Christmas,
this season's a wedding.

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:28.540
And it must be a fun show
to make, because obviously

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:31.076
you're someone who has always
seen the comedy

00:03:31.076 --> 00:03:33.145
in being part of a family.

00:03:33.145 --> 00:03:38.050
-Well, it was also interesting
because this second season

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.586
was built around the idea
of a wedding that goes bad,

00:03:40.586 --> 00:03:42.955
which everybody finds out
in the first like 15 seconds

00:03:42.955 --> 00:03:45.224
of the opening episode.

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:50.029
But because of the timing,
by the time we went to shoot it,

00:03:50.029 --> 00:03:51.664
the writers said,
"You know what we'll do is,

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:53.899
we'll make it sort of
just post-pandemic."

00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:57.270
So the idea was the kids
actually -- except for one --

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.273
had actually left,
and then they came back,

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:01.840
and then --
you know, for the pandemic --

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.276
and then they left again,
and then they came back again.

00:04:04.276 --> 00:04:06.545
So I actually
had a lot to draw on

00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:09.482
from my own experiences
with my own family.

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:10.883
And if you're watching
this show,

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.651
and the audience
will pick up on it,

00:04:12.651 --> 00:04:14.320
you know, it's the same thing.
They're just --

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.156
They're taking us for granted,
they're eating all our food.

00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:20.760
I mean --
You know, it's a funny thing.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.429
At a certain point, when I
was here with my own family,

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:25.164
because they were
eating everything,

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:28.267
as a grown man,
I was hiding cupcakes.

00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:29.368
I was doing that
in my own house.

00:04:29.368 --> 00:04:32.337
[ Laughter ]
I was hiding cupcakes

00:04:32.337 --> 00:04:34.640
from my adult children

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.143
because they were eating
all my cupcakes, you know?

00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:38.344
I mean,
going to the store was like --

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:39.912
You remember, in the beginning
of the pandemic,

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:42.215
that was like a scouting mission
in World War II.

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:44.483
You had to have
masks on and gloves.

00:04:44.483 --> 00:04:46.618
You know.
So when I went out to get

00:04:46.618 --> 00:04:49.021
the cupcakes, I bring back
what I thought was enough,

00:04:49.021 --> 00:04:50.723
you know, for everybody.

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:52.592
And then wake up,
and they were all gone.

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:54.727
So I hid cupcakes.

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:56.896
And I'm not proud of it,
but I did it.

00:04:56.896 --> 00:04:58.831
-I think there's that thing
that happens where people

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:01.267
sometimes will look at someone
like you and think,

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:03.402
"God, there's someone
who has it made.

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:04.904
An incredibly successful
career."

00:05:04.904 --> 00:05:06.973
And it's very nice for you
to share with them

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:10.643
that you're just a man like any
other who has to hide cupcakes.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:13.913
-Oh, you listen, and you --
you get -- I am so --

00:05:13.913 --> 00:05:15.581
I'm not cool as a dad
in the first place,

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:19.585
but then I get criticized for,
you know, everything.

00:05:19.585 --> 00:05:21.487
Everything I think about --
You know, like,

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:24.523
one of the things I did during
the course of the pandemic,

00:05:24.523 --> 00:05:26.659
and the voting on dinner

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:28.694
was they would get into
these conversations.

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:31.196
Voting on dinner,
for them, discussing it,

00:05:31.196 --> 00:05:33.199
would be like 45 minutes.

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:35.768
And then the idea
of picking something to watch

00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:37.102
was like an hour and a half

00:05:37.102 --> 00:05:40.105
just to find something
that they all wanted to watch.

00:05:40.105 --> 00:05:42.608
So I would just sneak off
and make a Paul Newman

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:44.176
frozen pizza,
which takes like 10 minutes,

00:05:44.176 --> 00:05:48.014
and watch a Paul Newman movie
in my -- in my own room.

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:49.281
In the bedroom.
I would.

00:05:49.281 --> 00:05:51.149
And, you know, it's like,

00:05:51.149 --> 00:05:54.019
Paul Newman got me through
the pandemic, basically.

00:05:54.019 --> 00:05:57.156
But I actually remember,
when my kids were small --

00:05:57.156 --> 00:06:01.861
this is, like, what fame
really -- how you sum it up.

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:04.630
My kids had grown up --
they're probably like 10, 12,

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:07.132
and there was a bunch of cousins
at my house this one night,

00:06:07.132 --> 00:06:08.967
and we were watching
a Paul Newman movie,

00:06:08.967 --> 00:06:10.535
and one of the kids goes,

00:06:10.535 --> 00:06:13.873
"Hey, that Paul Newman chef guy
was also an actor?"

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:15.074
And I was like,
"Yeah, there you go.

00:06:15.074 --> 00:06:16.775
That's Paul Newman's career."

00:06:16.775 --> 00:06:18.477
They thought he was a chef.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:18.477 --> 00:06:21.947
That's what fame means.
And, of course, it makes sense,

00:06:21.947 --> 00:06:25.050
'cause they're' looking at his
picture on frozen-pizza boxes

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.220
and salad-dressing bottles,
you know, so...

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:32.091
-My kids, of course, their
entire lives I've been on TV,

00:06:32.091 --> 00:06:34.360
and so I'll FaceTime with them

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.295
right before I come on
and do the show.

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:37.930
And I'll often be
in the makeup chair,

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:41.067
and you know that's something
that we just have to go through.

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:44.269
And today I FaceTimed
with my kid, and he had --

00:06:44.269 --> 00:06:46.004
a friend was behind him.

00:06:46.004 --> 00:06:48.274
And so my kid's not surprised
that I'm sitting there

00:06:48.274 --> 00:06:49.642
with somebody doing my hair,

00:06:49.642 --> 00:06:52.511
but I just watched his friend
in the background go...

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:55.881
[ Laughter ]

00:06:55.881 --> 00:06:58.150
Like, "What the hell
is your dad's deal?"

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:00.186
-"What the hell is going on?"

00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:02.120
-Hey, I do want
to give a shout-out.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.390
Chelsea Frei
plays your daughter on the show.

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:08.361
-Yeah.
-And Chelsea succeeded

00:07:08.361 --> 00:07:10.462
in spite of the fact
that she was an intern

00:07:10.462 --> 00:07:12.064
on this show
when we first started.

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:15.868
So we just want to give a
shout-out to her on her success,

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:18.537
and we're so happy that you two
are working together.

00:07:18.537 --> 00:07:20.973
-You know, it's so weird, Seth,
because I had no idea.

00:07:20.973 --> 00:07:24.944
She never told me that.
And so today, one of the people

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:26.345
on your staff mentioned it,
and I was like,

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:28.347
"You got to be kidding me."
So I called Chelsea,

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:29.849
and Chelsea said,
"Yeah, I loved it.

00:07:29.849 --> 00:07:31.683
It was the greatest,
blah blah blah."

00:07:31.683 --> 00:07:33.385
So, you know, it's just --

00:07:33.385 --> 00:07:36.756
It's such a small world in show
business anyways, you know?

00:07:36.756 --> 00:07:39.225
But that's just --
that's a point of fact,

00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:42.261
you know, how you discover
somebody who was working.

00:07:42.261 --> 00:07:43.763
I think she was like,
a music intern, right?

00:07:43.763 --> 00:07:45.564
-She was a music intern.
It's also --

00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:48.201
It really speaks to why anyone,
at any level,

00:07:48.201 --> 00:07:50.402
not just in show business
but anywhere,

00:07:50.402 --> 00:07:52.371
should be very nice
to their interns.

00:07:52.371 --> 00:07:53.905
Because they don't just --

00:07:53.905 --> 00:07:56.242
When it's over, they don't,
like, go back in a box.

00:07:56.242 --> 00:07:58.110
-That's exactly right.
-They like --

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.646
They continue on in life,
and they, like, maybe one day

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:02.915
will speak about
their experience.

00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:04.549
-Well, I got to
tell you something.

00:08:04.549 --> 00:08:06.786
I would have said this even if
she hadn't been intern

00:08:06.786 --> 00:08:09.188
at "The Seth Meyers Show,"
which I'm on tonight, right now.

00:08:09.188 --> 00:08:10.589
[ Laughter ]

00:08:10.589 --> 00:08:13.992
I got to tell you, man, she --
that entire cast is great.

00:08:13.992 --> 00:08:16.128
Elizabeth Perkins
and Jay Baruchel,

00:08:16.128 --> 00:08:17.896
I think everybody
was kind of aware of.

00:08:17.896 --> 00:08:20.032
You know, obviously, Elizabeth

00:08:20.032 --> 00:08:22.668
is an amazing comedy God,
in my book.

00:08:22.668 --> 00:08:25.171
And so is Jay.
But Chelsea's sort of like

00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:29.508
this surprise kind of unknown,
and she's unbelievably talented.

00:08:29.508 --> 00:08:31.643
Not only is she
a great comic actress,

00:08:31.643 --> 00:08:33.813
you know,
the show goes back and forth

00:08:33.813 --> 00:08:36.415
between primarily comedy
and some drama.

00:08:36.415 --> 00:08:38.150
She's a great dramatic actress.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:39.384
She's fantastic.

00:08:39.384 --> 00:08:41.687
I mean she's --
I can't say enough about her.

00:08:41.687 --> 00:08:43.088
I love her.
I love her.

00:08:43.088 --> 00:08:45.958
-Well, we're so happy --
We're so happy for her.

00:08:45.958 --> 00:08:47.893
And it's always so great
seeing you, Denis.

00:08:47.893 --> 00:08:49.361
Thanks so much for being here.

00:08:49.361 --> 00:08:51.931
-The last time I saw you live,
I think, was years ago.

00:08:51.931 --> 00:08:55.000
Once I'm vaxxed -- I'm fully
vaxxed as of tomorrow --

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.768
so the next time,
I'm gonna come in,

00:08:56.768 --> 00:08:58.170
hopefully, to the studio.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:00.138
-We would love to see you again.

00:09:00.138 --> 00:09:03.308
It's nice having you nearby,
and we'll see you then.

00:09:03.308 --> 00:09:05.210
-All right, pal, thanks.
-All right, be well.

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:07.346
"The Moodys" airs
Thursday nights on Fox.

